WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.190
- Welcome everyone to new coffee break,

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.200
here on my channel "Firm Learning".

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.190
My name is Heinrich.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.530
I'm a former McKinsey
consultant, and on my channel

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.660
I want to help you to become successful

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.640
in the first years of your career.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:12.540
And today I wanna share

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.700
with you 10 tips to communicate better,

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.640
and be perceived as
more senior on the job.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:21.000
Some of these tips are
quite sneaky, quite juicy.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.360
I can promise you that all
of them are highly effective.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.790
So let's get into it with tip number one.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.830
So, sometimes from a colleague or a client

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.330
you will hear an expression

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:32.580
or a concept that you don't know yet.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.670
And many people will just
ask, I don't know this,

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:36.600
what does this mean?

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.700
Can you please explain this to me?

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.110
And of course this implies

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.420
that you have no idea
about what this is about,

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:44.760
you might be perceived as
not really knowledgeable.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.200
So next time this happens to you,

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.210
try instead to say something like,

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.610
Wat does this expression mean to you?

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.710
What do you understand with this term?

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:53.910
Because this implies that actually

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.770
you know what this expression means,

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.900
but you even have this
higher level understanding

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.900
that sometimes different people
understand different things

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.720
with the same expression,
so you want to clarify it.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.000
And this takes away this
notion that you have no idea

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:06.990
what you're talking about.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.400
Next tip, we all know this

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.840
we already have lots of things to do,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.640
it may be already late in the evening,

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.290
and now your supervisor, your leadership,

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:14.781
your team leader approaches you

00:01:14.781 --> 00:01:16.200
and wants you to do even more.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.090
So what a junior person might reply

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.400
to this is something
like, I just cannot do it,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:21.990
I have too many things to do,

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.930
can you please ask someone
else to help you with that?

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.660
And of course, this usually
doesn't give the best vibes,

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.520
this doesn't show the best attitude.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.380
So instead, try saying something like,

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.660
sure, let's look into this together.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.170
Can you please help me
reprioritize my tasks

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:38.430
for the rest of the day?

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.130
Because this shows that yes,
you are willing to do it,

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.410
you just need the help
from the other person

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.900
to decide what the most
important things are

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:49.290
that need to get done today,
this evening, or this week.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.720
Next tip, a mistake that many people do is

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.350
that they end their sentences
always on a high pitch.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:59.280
So they go up with their voice
at the end of every sentence,

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.870
and this might look something like this.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.580
So you share an idea,

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.800
and then this leads to this other thing,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.260
and actually you also have
this other thing to share.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.690
And then there's also this
other aspect that's relevant

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.120
and na-na-na-na, na-na-na, na-na-na-na.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.500
So always at the end of every
sentence, the pitch goes up,

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.530
and this leaves the
people not really engaged.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.240
This takes away gravitas,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.600
takes away importance from
the things that you say.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:27.390
Instead, try to end every sentence

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:30.690
with your pitch of your voice going down.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.070
So say something like this.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.230
I have this very important idea

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.510
that today I wanna share with you.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:38.040
It is really something that will make

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.380
a big contribution to the work.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.950
At the end of every sentence,
go down with your pitch.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:46.920
This just adds gravitas
and underlines the points

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.390
that are really important.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.700
Next tip, you might be
faced with a situation

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.500
where you really don't know what to do.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:55.080
Maybe you get a task from
your leadership, but you have

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.590
no clue how to address this,
on how to structure this.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:01.200
Here, again, a more junior
person might just say,

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.960
I have no clue, I really
don't know what to do,

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.740
I feel completely lost, can
you please help me with that?

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:08.640
And again, of course,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.800
this often doesn't give
the best impression.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.960
Instead, try to say something like,

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.360
I would really appreciate your insights

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.030
in helping me shape this approach.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:19.380
Make it about the other person,

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.030
make it about the experience,

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:22.830
the insights of the other person, and now,

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.290
together, you will structure and solve

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.060
this maybe quite challenging task.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.190
Next tip is to focus on organization

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.380
and team leadership questions.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.070
Juniors in meetings often
very much focus on tasks,

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.950
on responsibilities, on
things that need to get done.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.350
Well, of course, that's
very important as well.

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.300
One tip I once got is that if you wanna

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:46.290
be perceived as more senior,
ask more questions regarding

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.660
the organization, the team leadership.

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.990
As a leader you are responsible
for enabling your team,

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:55.200
for making sure that your
team is set up for success.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.080
So ask questions exactly
in that direction.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.960
Do we have the right
capabilities on the team?

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.230
Is this person able to lead
the project in the right way?

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.610
Can this person actually lead

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:07.830
and supervise this more junior colleague?

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:09.960
Are we working together
with the right clients?

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.700
Are we set up for success?

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:13.740
So these are typical questions

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.080
that more senior people will ask.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:17.310
Try to do this yourself

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:19.110
if you wanna be perceived
this way as well.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:21.120
These were the first five of the 10 tips

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.350
I wanna share with you today.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.660
If you took any value out of them at all,

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.150
hit the like button to
help me with the algorithm.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.550
And of course, subscribe and
turn on the notification bell

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:31.320
to stay up to date with my content.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.480
But now, let's continue
with tip number six,

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.040
and this is a mistake that I often

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.780
see more junior leaders do.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.610
And this is that whenever
there's a meeting in front

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.100
of your own internal team,
or maybe also when you talk

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.830
to clients or other external stakeholders,

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:47.010
this leader wants to
present him or herself.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.930
The leader will want to give the result,

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:51.030
give the presentation by themselves,

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:53.790
and then often the team is
just sitting in the back,

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.370
listening to this junior leader present.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:58.890
Try to find a set-up
where you, as the leader,

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:02.670
will sit in the back and
actually your team presents,

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:05.520
maybe not everything, but
at least some sub chapters

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.310
of the overall presentation,
of the overall meeting.

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.040
Put them in the spotlight, let them shine.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.200
Of course, everyone knows
that you will not have

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.250
done everything yourself.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.440
It's clear that your team will have done

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.150
a good part of the work as well.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.940
So give them that exposure,
give them that spotlight.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.250
And this also makes you to be perceived

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:26.790
as a good people leader, a
good people leader who trusts

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.890
in his or her team, a
people leader who wants

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.230
to develop his team members.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.300
This will be perceived
as more senior as well.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.180
Next, let's say you want to
praise someone for their job,

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.260
for their work.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:38.910
And here, many people will just say,

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:42.060
hey, you did a good job,
thumbs up, great work.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.860
It's great what you have accomplished.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.530
But of course this is
not bad, it's not wrong.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:48.660
Try to say something like,

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.260
thank you for your leadership
on this initiative.

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.870
Thank people for their leadership.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:56.940
This is something that
I've seen very often

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:58.290
more senior people do.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.720
And this of course instills also the idea

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.420
in more junior colleagues
that they are not just here

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:06.660
to get some work done,
but they are here to lead.

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.760
And maybe just one small initiative,

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.210
maybe just a sub part
of the overall project,

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:14.340
but you want people to take ownership,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:18.780
take responsibility, lead
forward, make things work.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.300
So stress this with your words
whenever you give feedback

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:22.980
and give praise to your colleagues

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.740
Next tip, many colleagues,
especially more junior ones,

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.960
don't really communicate that much.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.420
They will not proactively
reach out to leadership

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:33.450
to share some points, maybe
in one-on-one team meetings

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.400
this is where might say something.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.500
What I've seen many project leads do

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.870
at companies like McKinsey, BCG, Bain,

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:42.600
is that at the end of
every week they will write

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.870
an end of week email sharing three things.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:48.000
First, the highlights of the last week,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.950
the lowlights of the last week,

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:52.470
and the priorities for the next week,

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:54.060
and they will send this to the whole team

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.620
and their leadership.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.480
Be a proactive communicator.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.610
Share your thoughts, be transparent,

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:01.800
also things that haven't gone that great.

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.480
This as well will make you be perceived

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:04.530
as more senior as well.

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:07.590
Next tip, when someone wants
to communicate an update

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:10.290
on a project or any other
initiative or train of thought

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.650
that they're having, it
will often go like this,

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:15.210
so yeah, this is what I think,

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:17.490
and this is because of this reason.

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:19.470
And then there's also this other reason,

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:22.380
and, ah ya, this other
reason is important as well.

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:24.930
So don't do that, don't
communicate like this.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:27.960
This will be perceived
as very unstructured,

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.410
instead, leverage the rule of three.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:32.653
If you don't know what that it is,

00:07:32.653 --> 00:07:34.470
I highly encourage you
to watch another video

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:36.150
that I will insert somewhere above here

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:37.770
where discuss this in detail.

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:40.230
But the basic idea is that
you should start always

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.510
by saying something like,
this is what I hold true

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:44.430
and it's because of three reasons.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:46.260
So you always start by saying

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:48.330
that there are three
reasons for something.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:50.850
First, second, and third.

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:52.980
Very easy structure, very effective.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.930
Check out the other
video if you have no idea

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:56.070
what this might make sense.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:08:01.070
Next tip, don't talk too fast,
and strategically use breaks,

00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:03.150
make use of silence.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:06.060
Many junior people just talk far too fast.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:09.030
Talking fast is usually
perceived as being nervous

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:11.130
and not being safe, secure,

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.860
instead, strategically use silence.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.800
Just sit there and say
nothing, especially after,

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:18.450
for instance, a client interview,

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:19.830
explained a couple of things

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.720
and you wanna actually hear even more.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:23.403
Just say nothing.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.840
Because for many people,
this will be uncomfortable

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:31.560
and often will instill the
other person to share even more.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:32.880
But now I'd love to hear from you.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:34.920
Do you have any other additional tips

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:36.180
that you'd like to share?

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:37.830
Leave a comment with your insights,

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:39.420
this would be highly appreciated.

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:42.510
Big, big, thanks to all
the members of the channel.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:43.950
Thanks so much for your support.

